          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong nuclear vector space
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+convex+topological+vector+space"} locally convex topological vector space
            that is as far from being a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+vector+space"} normed vector space
            as possible. Any map from a nuclear space into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+vector+space"} normed vector space
            is compact, whence the only normed nuclear spaces are finite dimensional.
          %p
            Nuclear spaces have very good properties with regard to
            = succeed "s" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                topological tensor product
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/topological+tensor+product"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+vector+space"} duality
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            To define a nuclear space we need to start with the concept of a nuclear map, first between
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+spaces"} Banach spaces
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+spaces"} Banach spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}(E,F)
            be the Banach space of continuous linear maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*
            denote the dual Banach space of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ˜
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^* \widetilde{\otimes} F
            denote the completion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+tensor+product"} projective tensor product
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo *
                  %mo ×
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^* \times F \to \mathcal{L}(E,F)
            extends to a continuous linear map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ˜
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^* \widetilde{\otimes} F \to \mathcal{L}(E,F)
            (which might not be injective).
          #nnmap.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              be Banach spaces. A linear map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon E \to F
              is
              %strong nuclear
              if it lies in the image in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}(E,F)
              of the completion of the projective tensor product
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi E
                        %mo *
                      %mover
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mo ˜
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^* \widetilde{\otimes} F
          %p
            From the notion of nuclear maps between Banach spaces we can define nuclear maps between arbitrary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LCTVS"} LCTVS
          %p
            To make this precise, we need to recall how to associate Banach spaces to certain subsets of an LCTVS. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            be an LCTVS and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq E
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+set"} convex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circled+set"} circled
            = succeed "-neighbourhood." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi E
                      %mo ˜
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widetilde{E}_U
            as follows: as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            is convex and circled, its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+functional"} Minkowski functional
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-norm"} semi-norm
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi U
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ker
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_U \coloneqq E/\ker U
            is therefore a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+vector+space"} normed vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            is a
            = succeed "-neighbourhood," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to E_U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{E}_U
            to be the Banach completion of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_U
          %p
            There is a dual notion. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            be an LCTVS and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \subseteq F
            a convex, circled, and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+subset"} bounded subset
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_B
            be the span of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absorbing"} absorbing
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_B
            and so its Minkowski functional is defined. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff"} Hausdorff
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            cannot contain a linear subspace and thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_B
            is a normed vector space. We cannot complete
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_B
            to a Banach space but it might so happen that it is one. As
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is bounded, the inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_B \to F
            is continuous.
          %p
            (There is no danger of confusing the two notations since if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            admits a bounded
            = succeed "-neighbourhood" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
          %p
            Now we say that a continuous linear map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon E \to F
            is
            %strong bounded
            if for some
            = succeed "-neighbourhood" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            (which we may take to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circled+set"} circled
            and
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+set"} convex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(U)
            is bounded in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            factors through a continuous map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{U,B} \colon E_U \to F_B
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \subseteq F
            is bounded and contains
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(U)
          #nmap.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              be LCTVS. A linear map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon E \to F
              is
              %strong nuclear
              if there exists a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+set"} convex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circled+set"} circled
              = succeed "-neighbourhood," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+set"} convex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circled+set"} circled
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+set"} bounded
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_B
              complete such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(U) \subseteq B
              and the associated map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{U,B} \colon \tilde{E}_U \to F_B
              is nuclear.
          %p The following characterisation of nuclear maps is often helpful.
          #nchar.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              A linear map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon E \to F
              is nuclear if and only if it is of the form:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mn ∞
                    %msub
                      %mi λ
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    u(x) = \sum_{n=1}^\infty \lambda_n f_n(x) y_n
            %p
              where
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %mn ∞
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %msub
                          %mi λ
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{n=1}^\infty {|\lambda_n|} \lt \infty
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{f_n\}
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equicontinuous"} equicontinuous
              sequence in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi E
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^*
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{y_n\}
              is a sequence in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              contained in a convex, circled, bounded subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_B
              is complete.
          %p Now that we have the notion of a nuclear map, we can define a nuclear space.
          #nsp.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LCTVS"} LCTVS
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              is nuclear if it has a base
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              of convex circled
              = succeed "-neighbourhoods" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \in \mathcal{B}
              the canonical mapping
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to \tilde{E}_V
              is nuclear.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ol
            %li
              The following are equivalent:
              %ol
                %li
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                  is nuclear,
                %li
                  Every continuous linear map of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                  into any Banach space is nuclear,
                %li
                  Every convex, circled
                  = succeed "-neighbourhood" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                  contains another, say
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi V
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mover
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mi V
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mover
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{E}_V \to \tilde{E}_U
                  is nuclear.
            %li Every bounded subset of a nuclear space is precompact.
            %li The completion of a nuclear space is a nuclear space.
            %li
              A nuclear space is a projective limit of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℓ
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell^p
              spaces (in particular, of
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            %li Nuclearity is inherited by the following constructions: subspaces, separated quotients, arbitrary products, locally convex direct sum of a countable family, projective limits, countable inductive limits.
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+tensor+product"} projective tensor product
              (and its completion) of two nuclear spaces is nuclear.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ol
            %li Any finite dimensional vector space is nuclear,
            %li
              For a finite dimensional smooth manifold
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(M)
              is nuclear,
            %li
              The space of rapidly decreasing functions on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              is nuclear,
            %li
              The product an arbitrary number of copies of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              is nuclear,
            %li
              The direct sum of a countable number of copies of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              is nuclear,
            %li
              The direct sum of
              = succeed "-copies" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              is
              %em not
              nuclear.
